.SH Message rendering
\f[CR]mail_parse.{c,h}\f[R] consumes the output of
\f[CR]notmuch show \-\-format=text\f[R] and produces a clean
per\-message section:
.IP
.EX
From:    Alice <alice\[at]example.com>
To:      bob\[at]example.com
Cc:      team\[at]example.com
Subject: Quarterly review
Date:    Tue, 18 May 2026 10:14:00 +0200
Attachments:  [2] notes.pdf  [3] chart.png

<body>
.EE
.IP \[bu] 2
Headers are normalized to the columns shown above; missing fields are
simply omitted.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[CR]Attachments:\f[R] line appears right below \f[CR]Date:\f[R]
only when the message has attachments.
Each entry is \f[CR][<notmuch part id>] <name>\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Multi\-message threads render every message as its own section with a
horizontal\-rule divider between them.
.IP \[bu] 2
The body always prefers \f[CR]text/plain\f[R] if present.
When only HTML is available, it is piped through
\f[CR]w3m \-dump \-T text/html\f[R] (or \f[CR]lynx\f[R] if w3m is
missing).
With neither available, a placeholder line is shown.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]\[rs]fmessage{\f[R], \f[CR]\[rs]fheader{\f[R],
\f[CR]\[rs]fpart{\f[R] and other notmuch framing markers never reach the
buffer.
.PP
The highlighter (\f[CR]mail_msg_hl\f[R]) styles header keys, header
values, quoted lines (\f[CR]>\f[R]), the \f[CR]Attachments:\f[R]
pseudo\-header, and the section divider.
.SH Configuration
All knobs go in \f[CR]src/config.c\f[R] after
\f[CR]plugin_load(&plugin_mail, 1)\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]\[dq]mail/mail.h\[dq]\f[R]
\&...
plugin_load(&plugin_mail, 1);

mail_set_dir(\[dq]/home/user/.mail\[dq]);              \f[I]/* maildir root        */\f[R]
mail_set_query(\[dq]tag:inbox AND NOT tag:muted\[dq]); \f[I]/* default base query  */\f[R]
mail_set_mbsync_profile(\[dq]personal\[dq]);           \f[I]/* default: \[dq]\-a\[dq]       */\f[R]
mail_set_send_cmd(\[dq]msmtp \-t \-a personal\[dq]);     \f[I]/* default: \[dq]msmtp \-t\[dq] */\f[R]
mail_set_from(\[dq]Me <me\[at]example.com>\[dq]);          \f[I]/* From: in compose    */\f[R]

\f[I]/* Saved views shown at the top of the mailbox sidebar. */\f[R]
mail_add_view(\[dq]Unread\[dq],      \[dq]tag:unread\[dq]);
mail_add_view(\[dq]Today\[dq],       \[dq]date:today..\[dq]);
mail_add_view(\[dq]From boss\[dq],   \[dq]from:boss\[at]example.com\[dq]);
.EE
.SH Mailbox sidebar
\f[CR]:mail\-mailboxes\f[R] (or \f[CR]b\f[R] from the list) scans
\f[CR]mail_get_dir()\f[R] and shows:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR][All mail]\f[R] \[em] clears both the base query and the mailbox
scope.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]── Views ──\f[R] \[em] every entry registered with
\f[CR]mail_add_view\f[R].
Selecting one sets the base query.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]── Mailboxes ──\f[R] \[em] accounts (top\-level dirs under the
maildir root) and their folders.
Selecting one sets a \f[CR]folder:\&...\f[R] or \f[CR]path:\&...\f[R]
scope that is ANDed with the base query.
.PP
Two layouts are supported: a single maildir
(\f[CR]\[ti]/.mail/cur,new\f[R]) or a collection of accounts
(\f[CR]\[ti]/.mail/<account>/<folder>/cur,new\f[R]).
.SH Compose / reply / forward
\f[CR]:mail\-compose\f[R] opens a buffer pre\-filled with
\f[CR]From:\f[R], \f[CR]To:\f[R], \f[CR]Cc:\f[R], \f[CR]Subject:\f[R]
and lands you in insert mode on the To: line.
\f[CR]:mail\-send\f[R] pipes the buffer (validated to have non\-empty
\f[CR]To:\f[R] and \f[CR]Subject:\f[R]) to the configured send command
via a temp file, so msmtp/sendmail still inherit a controlling terminal
for password prompts.
.PP
\f[CR]:mail\-reply\f[R] / \f[CR]:mail\-reply\-all\f[R] use
\f[CR]notmuch reply\f[R] to generate the quoted draft; the configured
\f[CR]mail_set_from\f[R] (if any) overrides the \f[CR]From:\f[R] line
produced by notmuch.
.PP
\f[CR]:mail\-forward\f[R] builds a clean compose:
\f[CR]From: <your address>\f[R], \f[CR]To:\f[R] (left empty for you to
fill), \f[CR]Cc:\f[R] empty, and \f[CR]Subject: Fwd: <original>\f[R]
(unless the source subject already starts with
\f[CR]Fwd:\f[R]/\f[CR]Fw:\f[R]).
Below the blank line goes a
\f[CR]\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- Forwarded message \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\f[R]
block with the original \f[CR]From\f[R] / \f[CR]Date\f[R] /
\f[CR]Subject\f[R] / \f[CR]To\f[R] / \f[CR]Cc\f[R] headers and the body
lines copied straight out of the message buffer the user is reading.
Any attachments on the source message are extracted to a per\-forward
\f[CR]/tmp/hed\-mail\-fwd\-\&.../\f[R] directory and listed as
\f[CR]Attach:\f[R] pseudo\- headers in the compose buffer;
\f[CR]:mail\-send\f[R] consumes those at send time and emits a real
\f[CR]multipart/mixed\f[R] MIME message (text body part +
base64\-encoded attachment parts, with mime type sniffed via the
\f[CR]file\f[R] command, falling back to
\f[CR]application/octet\-stream\f[R]).
.PP
You can delete \f[CR]Attach:\f[R] lines from the compose to drop
individual attachments, or add new \f[CR]Attach: /path/to/file\f[R]
lines anywhere in the header block to attach extra files.
\f[CR]:mail\-attach\-add [path]\f[R] (or \f[CR]<C\-c><C\-a>\f[R]) does
that for you: with a path it validates and inserts the line; without one
it opens an fzf multi\-pick over project files.
Without any \f[CR]Attach:\f[R] headers, \f[CR]:mail\-send\f[R] produces
a plain\-text RFC 822 message exactly as before.
.PP
\f[CR]<C\-c><C\-c>\f[R] in the compose buffer sends directly (the mutt /
Emacs message\-mode convention), equivalent to \f[CR]:mail\-send\f[R].
.SH Attachments
Attachments are detected at parse time from the notmuch text stream
\[em] no second pass over the buffer.
\f[CR]:mail\-attach\f[R] (or \f[CR]a\f[R] in a message buffer) extracts
each part with \f[CR]notmuch show \-\-part=<id> \-\-format=raw\f[R] to
\f[CR]/tmp/hed\-mail\-<id>\-<safename>\f[R] and opens it via the
\f[CR]open\f[R] plugin.
With multiple attachments, an fzf picker shows \f[CR][id] name\f[R] for
each part \[em] \f[CR]Tab\f[R] toggles a row, \f[CR]<C\-a>\f[R] selects
all; every picked part is opened.
You can still bypass the picker by passing an explicit id
(\f[CR]:mail\-attach 3\f[R]).
.PP
\f[CR]:mail\-attach save [id] [dir]\f[R] (or \f[CR]A\f[R] in a message
buffer) goes through the same picker but writes the picked parts into
\f[CR]dir\f[R] (default \f[CR]\[ti]/Downloads\f[R], created if needed)
instead of opening them.
